{{Short description|Australian opinion polling}}
{{Use Australian English|date=December 2020}}
{{Use dmy dates|date=February 2022}}
{{2022 Australian federal election sidebar}}
In the lead-up to the [[2022 Australian federal election]], a number of polling companies have been conducting regular [[opinion poll]]s for various news organisations. These polls collected data on parties' primary vote, and contained an estimation of the [[two-party-preferred vote]]. They also asked questions about the electorates' views on major party leaders. Key polling companies are [[YouGov]], [[Essential Media Communications]], [[Roy Morgan Research]], and Resolve Strategic.

[[Ipsos Australia|Ipsos]] polls used to be published in ''[[The Sydney Morning Herald]]'', ''[[The Age]]'' and the ''[[Australian Financial Review]]'';<ref name=inside2019>{{cite web | title=Who controls opinion polling in Australia, what else we need to know about the polls, and why it matters | website=Inside Story | date=15 May 2019 | url=https://insidestory.org.au/who-controls-opinion-polling-in-australia/  |archive-url=https://ghostarchive.org/archive/20220211/https://insidestory.org.au/who-controls-opinion-polling-in-australia/  |archive-date=2022-02-11 |url-status=live| access-date=11 February 2022}}{{cbignore}}</ref><ref name=ownership>{{cite web | title=For the Record: Ownership of Australian Public Opinion Polling and Market Research Companies| first1= Gary |last1= Morgan |first2= Michele| last2= Levine | website=[[Roy Morgan Research]] | date=11 April 2019 | url=http://www.roymorgan.com/findings/7943-ownership-of-australian-public-opinion-polling-and-market-research-companies-201904110526  |archive-url=https://ghostarchive.org/archive/20220211/http://www.roymorgan.com/findings/7943-ownership-of-australian-public-opinion-polling-and-market-research-companies-201904110526  |archive-date=2022-02-11 |url-status=live| access-date=11 February 2022}}{{cbignore}}</ref> however following the shock result of the [[2019 Australian federal election]], when the [[Coalition (Australia)|Coalition]] won the election against all of the opinion polls' predictions, the [[Nine Entertainment]] group decided to discontinue its relationship with that company.<ref>{{cite web | last=Wahlquist | first=Calla | title=Sydney Morning Herald and Age to stop running Ipsos poll after surprise election result | website=the Guardian | date=23 May 2019 | url=http://www.theguardian.com/media/2019/may/24/sydney-morning-herald-and-age-to-stop-running-ipsos-poll-after-surprise-election-result | access-date=12 February 2022}}</ref> The newspapers did not report any Ipsos political polling until 4 April 2022,<ref name="afr-ipsos">{{cite news |last1=Coorey |first1=Phillip |title=Labor leads in the biggest states, says the latest Ipsos poll |url=https://www.afr.com/politics/federal/labor-leads-in-all-states-and-territories-poll-20220404-p5aakq |access-date=5 April 2022 |work=Australian Financial Review |date=4 April 2022}}</ref> but continued to report the results of other Ipsos polls.<ref name=smhtopic>{{cite web | last=Singhal | first=Pallavi | last2=Topsfield | first2=Jewel | title=Topic – Ipsos poll | website=[[The Sydney Morning Herald]] | date=5 February 2021 | url=https://www.smh.com.au/topic/ipsos-poll-jks | access-date=12 February 2022}}</ref>

